I changed `filter-users.ts` so it no longer compares `user.role` with `===`. I did not run tests, lint or the app, as you asked.

The violation was the role filter line `if (role !== 'ALL' && user.role !== role) return false;`. It now builds the set of roles to keep and tests membership, the same pattern `checkAccess` uses in `@/lib/authorization`:

```ts
const ALL_ROLES: User['role'][] = [ROLES.ADMIN, ROLES.USER];
// ...
const allowedRoles: User['role'][] = role === 'ALL' ? ALL_ROLES : [role];
// ...
if (!allowedRoles.includes(user.role)) return false;
```

I also imported `ROLES` from `@/lib/authorization`. A PostToolUse lint hook flagged the literal `'ADMIN'`/`'USER'` strings in my first version, so the roles are written as `ROLES.ADMIN` and `ROLES.USER`.

One judgment call for you: this is a list filter, not an access gate, so I treated it as in scope for the rule's intent. The code still reads `user.role`, just not in a comparison. If the reviewer means no direct reads of `user.role` at all, the role filter has to move out of the client. That would mean `getUsers` taking a role query parameter from the API layer, which I did not change.
